WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.838
Shell scripts are often introduced as
small pieces of glue code,

2
00:00:05.918 --> 00:00:09.773
but many of them become operational
software.

3
00:00:09.853 --> 00:00:15.785
They deploy applications, rotate logs,
copy files, restart services,

4
00:00:15.865 --> 00:00:21.104
run backups, collect diagnostics, move
data between systems,

5
00:00:21.184 --> 00:00:23.853
and support incident response.

6
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:27.689
When a script controls real
infrastructure,

7
00:00:27.769 --> 00:00:33.602
a small parsing mistake or unchecked
assumption can become an outage,

8
00:00:33.682 --> 00:00:38.525
a failed deployment, or a confusing
production incident.

9
00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:44.537
Shell is easy to underestimate because
simple commands look readable.

10
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:50.251
The hard parts are usually the invisible
ones: quoting, expansion,

11
00:00:50.331 --> 00:00:56.658
word splitting, globbing, exit status,
command substitution, pipelines,

12
00:00:56.738 --> 00:01:00.494
and the differences between shell
dialects.

13
00:01:00.574 --> 00:01:05.912
A script can appear correct with one
filename, one environment,

14
00:01:05.992 --> 00:01:11.923
or one test value, then behave differently
when a path contains a space,

15
00:01:12.003 --> 00:01:16.056
a variable is empty, a wildcard appears in
input,

16
00:01:16.136 --> 00:01:19.695
or the script runs under a different
shell.

17
00:01:19.775 --> 00:01:26.497
ShellCheck helps by applying static
analysis to shell scripts before they reach

18
00:01:26.577 --> 00:01:27.665
production.

19
00:01:27.745 --> 00:01:33.577
It looks for patterns that commonly lead
to bugs, confusing behavior,

20
00:01:33.657 --> 00:01:37.611
portability problems, and unsafe
automation.

21
00:01:37.691 --> 00:01:43.919
It can flag likely quoting problems,
suspicious tests, unused variables,

22
00:01:43.999 --> 00:01:50.622
dialect mismatches, and other issues that
reviewers may miss during a busy pull

23
00:01:50.702 --> 00:01:51.493
request.

24
00:01:51.573 --> 00:01:53.748
The value is consistency:

25
00:01:53.828 --> 00:02:00.648
the same class of mistake can be caught
repeatedly across teams and repositories.

26
00:02:00.728 --> 00:02:06.758
ShellCheck does not replace engineering
judgment, testing, or review.

27
00:02:06.838 --> 00:02:13.560
It cannot know every operational context
or decide what failure should mean for a

28
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.321
specific workflow.

29
00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:20.442
The right posture is tool-assisted review,
not blind trust.

30
00:02:20.522 --> 00:02:25.663
A finding is a prompt to understand the
script more carefully.

31
00:02:25.743 --> 00:02:32.663
Used well, ShellCheck reduces preventable
failures, improves maintainability,

32
00:02:32.743 --> 00:02:39.366
and gives teams a practical way to make
shell automation safer before it matters

33
00:02:39.446 --> 00:02:39.940
most.
